% Trechos que corroboram:
% "There are no rich country cities with population above 5m without a subway system and subways are common even among rich country cities with populations in the 1 million–5 million range. Subways are relatively rare among developing country cities with populations less than about 5m and their frequency increases more or less smoothly with city size." (p. 92)
% "Two features of Table 1 stand out. First, the huge gap in subway provision between Europe and the rest of the world. Second, the weak connection between mean city size and subway extent. In particular, Asia is home to the preponderance of the world's large cities while South America's cities are larger, on average, than those elsewhere. However, neither South America nor Asia is well provided with subways relative to Europe and North America." (p. 91)
% Por que funciona: o argumento é mecânico — se os sistemas de transporte de massa estão concentrados em economias avançadas (Europa, EUA, e em menor grau Ásia desenvolvida), então o universo amostral disponível para estudos de capitalização também está.

